e).**\nA normal index on `users.email` can't be used when the column is wrapped in a function. The database will scan the whole `users` table and apply `lower()` to every row. Pick one of these fixes:\n- Create an expression index: `CREATE INDEX idx_users_email_lower ON users (lower(email));`. Postgres, Oracle, MySQL 8.0.13+ and SQLite support this. SQL Server needs a computed column with an index on it.\n- Store emails normalized to lowercase, then compare directly: `WHERE u.email = 'a@example.org'`. Back that with a unique index on `email`.\n- Use a case-insensitive type or collation, such as Postgres `citext` or MySQL's default `_ci` collations. Then a plain index works.\n\n**2. `SELECT *` pulls every column from both tables.**\n- It sends more data than needed and rules out index-only scans.\n- `id` and `user_id` appear twice, and other column names may collide, which can break clients that map results by name.\n- Name only the columns you need.\n\n**3. `orders.user_id` needs an index.**\nOnce the lookup finds the single user, the join needs to find that user's orders quickly. Without an index on `orders(user_id)`, the database may scan all of `orders`. Many databases don't create this index automatically for a foreign key. Postgres doesn't, and MySQL/InnoDB does.\n\n**4. There's no `LIMIT` or pagination.**\nIf a user can have many orders, the result set is unbounded. Add `ORDER BY o.created_at DESC LIMIT n`, ideally with a composite index on `orders(user_id, created_at)`.\n\n**Suggested rewrite** (assuming emails are stored lowercase and `email` is uniquely indexed):\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.created_at, o.total, u.email\nFROM users u\nJOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.id\nWHERE u.email = 'a@example.org'\nORDER BY o.created_at DESC\nLIMIT 50;\n```\n\nI only had the query text. I haven't seen the schema, existing indexes, row counts or an `EXPLAIN` plan, so the impact of each point is unconfirmed. Run `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` on the current and rewritten versions to check. The column names in the rewrite (`created_at`, `total`) are placeholders.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}